Aerie-Purpose: Report the graphics card as a vendor family in Chrome's own format (WebGL UNMASKED_VENDOR/RENDERER) and give WebGPU adapter info empty vendor, architecture, device and description, so the card model and driver are no fingerprint surface (issue #112).
Aerie-Upstream-Area: third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgl/webgl_rendering_context_base.cc (getParameter), third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu_adapter.cc (CreateAdapterInfoForAdapter), third_party/blink/public/common/helium_noise/ (HeliumNoiseFeature::kWebGL), content/browser/helium_noise/noise_token_data.cc, third_party/blink/common/features.cc (kHeliumNoiseWebGL)

HeliumNoiseFeature::kWebGL is a new surface token. Like the others it is derived per site
and session and sent only when protection is on for the site, so "token present" means
"protected here" and the per-site switch and helium.noise.enabled turn this surface off
without a renderer change. The new feature kHeliumNoiseWebGL (on by default) switches the
surface off for the Bench's negative control.

The two getParameter branches that read the ungoogled-chromium SpoofWebGLInfo flag are
replaced by one helper, UnmaskedGpuString: the flag, a value the user chose, still wins;
otherwise a protected context gets aerie_gpu_info::MaskedVendor/MaskedRenderer for the
family of the real strings, and an unprotected one gets the real strings. The family
mapping, the strings and the decision (ShouldMaskGpuInfo) are pure code with unit tests in
overlay/components/aerie_gpu_info. WebGPU adapter info goes through the same decision; the
driver string, which only developer features expose, is emptied too.

A worker has no noise tokens yet, so it masks whenever the features are on (fail closed).
Until issue #113 gives workers tokens, a worker started from a site that has protection
off therefore still sees masked values.

